EXT. HIGH DESERT - LONE CABIN - MORNING

A silver wind combs the sage. A tin chimney leans. ELIJAH FARROW, 50s, sits on the porch step in a sun-faded hat, a tin cup steaming in his hands. His horse dozes at the post, tail flicking, while a crow on the fence rail considers him with one black eye, the wind rattles a loose shutter, the dog's shadow slides across the dust, and Elijah sips, tips back his hat, and waits for something he doesn't name.

ELIJAH
(V.O.)
Dear Ruth. The roof held through the storm.

He sets the cup down. A faraway drone. A rider, small, grows on the horizon.

ELIJAH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I expect I'll have company soon. Don't worry. I've kept the kettle on.

The rider comes closer, and the crow lifts off the rail. The horse lifts its head. Elijah stands slowly, wipes his hands on his trousers, and walks inside.

ELIJAH (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Well.

He lifts the shotgun from its pegs above the door and breaks it open. Two shells. He thumbs them in. He closes the gun with a soft, sure click.

EXT. LONE CABIN - PORCH - CONTINUOUS

The rider reins up at the gate. Elijah steps onto the boards, shotgun low. He tips his hat, and a bright, gentle smile crosses his face.

ELIJAH
Morning, Ruth.
